\title{Transitive Closure}
\taxon{Example}
\import{prelude}

\p{We can use initial models in order to take the transitive closure of a relation, deriving further ancestry facts from those already recorded:}

\pre{%
\kw{theory} Family := \kw{sig}
  Person : Set
  is-descendant-of : Person -> Person -> Prop
\kw{end}

\kw{theory} TransitiveExtension (@ind m : Family) := \kw{sig}
  is-descendant-of/closure : m.Person -> m.Person -> Prop
  include : (x y : m.Person) ->
    m.is-descendant-of x y ->
    is-descendant-of/closure x y
  transitive : (x y z : m.Person) ->
    is-descendant-of/closure x y ->
    is-descendant-of/closure y z ->
    is-descendant-of/closure x z
\kw{end}

\kw{ind} \kw{def} TransitiveClosure (m : Family) := \kw{init} (TransitiveExtension m)
}

\p{Given a model \code{m} of \code{Family}, we can then access the transitive closure of \code{m.is-descendant-of} via \code{TransitiveClosure m .is-descendant-of/closure}. The intuition here is that \code{\kw{init} (TransitiveExtension m)} give us the minimal binary relation that both extends \code{m.is-descendant-of} and also is transitive; this is precisely the transitive closure.}

\p{We discuss why \code{@ind m} is necessary shortly.}
